## Startup Synchronization

Every repository task starts by reading `AGENTS.md` and checking Git consistency as one required step. The startup check covers worktree status, remotes, remote pruning when needed, and the relationship between `HEAD`, local `main`, and `backup/main`.

Implementation, commit, push, and PR creation do not proceed until that startup synchronization is complete.

After a PR is merged by the user, the next repository task treats post-merge cleanup as part of startup synchronization. The cleanup verifies the merged PR state, fetches `backup --prune`, fast-forwards local `main` to `backup/main`, and deletes only the matching merged local branch whose head is already contained in `backup/main`.

Post-merge cleanup is scoped to the matching merged branch for the PR just handled. Older unrelated local branches, including branches whose upstream was already pruned before the current task, are not deleted or treated as release blockers unless the user explicitly makes them part of the task.

## Technical Baseline

- TypeScript assumes the Deno runtime.
- Standard adoption libraries are limited to Deno standard libraries when needed.
- npm compatibility packages, npm dependencies, Node.js dependencies, external frontend frameworks, CSS preprocessors, CSS minification, CSS bundling, `dist/`, `build/`, and `node_modules/` are prohibited.
- CSS and JavaScript are managed as separate outputs.
- Generated outputs are kept in the repository and checked against local rules.

